# Hidden interview questions, Lessons 0–10. Q = [(lesson, level, question, trap, answer)]
# level: 1 = basics, 2 = typical interview, 3 = senior / deep-dive. Every number was checked with Python.
Q = [
 (0, 2, "You compute MSE with <code>np.mean((y_pred - y)**2)</code> and get 2.33, but by hand it's 0.33. What went wrong?",
  "The formula is right, so people check the maths.",
  "A shape bug. If <code>y_pred</code> has shape (3, 1) and <code>y</code> has shape (3,), broadcasting turns <code>y_pred - y</code> into a 3×3 matrix of every prediction minus every target. For predictions 1, 2, 3 and targets 1, 2, 4 that gives 2.33 instead of 0.33. Flatten with <code>.ravel()</code> and check <code>.shape</code> before computing losses."),
 (0, 1, "Why does <code>0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3</code> return False, and what should you do instead?",
  "Assuming it's a Python bug.",
  "Floats are stored in binary, where 0.1 can't be represented exactly; the sum is 0.30000000000000004. Compare with a tolerance: <code>np.isclose(a, b)</code> or <code>abs(a - b) &lt; 1e-9</code>. This matters when testing model outputs or gradients."),
 (0, 2, "You slice a NumPy array, edit the slice, and your original data changes. Why?",
  "Expecting Python list behaviour.",
  "NumPy slices are <em>views</em> sharing the same memory. Use <code>.copy()</code> when you need an independent array. Python list slices, by contrast, are copies."),
 (0, 2, "pandas warns <em>SettingWithCopyWarning</em> after <code>df[df.age &gt; 30]['score'] = 0</code>. Did the data change?",
  "Ignoring warnings.",
  "Probably not: the first indexing may return a copy, and the assignment changes the copy. Use one <code>.loc</code> call: <code>df.loc[df.age &gt; 30, 'score'] = 0</code>."),
 (0, 1, "What's the shape of <code>a.T</code> if <code>a</code> has shape (3,)?",
  "Expecting (3, 1).",
  "Still (3,). A 1-D array has no rows or columns to swap. Use <code>a.reshape(-1, 1)</code> or <code>a[:, None]</code> for a column vector."),

 (1, 1, "Is linear regression machine learning, or \"just statistics\"?",
  "Thinking ML means something complicated.",
  "It's both. It learns its parameters from data to make predictions, which is exactly what ML is. Statistics and ML share many models; they differ more in goals (explaining vs predicting) than in methods."),
 (1, 1, "What's the difference between a parameter and a hyperparameter? Give two examples of each.",
  "Calling the learning rate a parameter.",
  "Parameters are learned from data (linear-regression weights, a tree's split thresholds). Hyperparameters are set before training and tuned on validation data (learning rate, $k$ in kNN, maximum tree depth, regularisation strength $\\lambda$)."),
 (1, 2, "Your model gets 100% accuracy on the training data. Is that good news?",
  "Celebrating.",
  "Not by itself. It may have memorised the training data (overfitting), or a feature may leak the answer. Only a held-out validation/test score tells you how it generalises; also check the trivial baseline."),
 (1, 2, "Why do we train with one function (the loss) but report another (the metric)?",
  "Assuming they're the same thing.",
  "The loss must be smooth for gradient-based optimisation (log loss, MSE). The metric should reflect the real goal (F1, recall at a fixed precision, profit) and is often not differentiable, so you can't optimise it directly."),
 (1, 2, "Why squared error rather than absolute error?",
  "\"Because it's standard.\"",
  "Squared error is differentiable everywhere and has a closed-form solution, and the best constant prediction under it is the mean. Absolute error is more robust to outliers (its best constant is the median) but has a kink at 0. Squared error also punishes big misses far more: an error of 3 costs 9 times an error of 1."),
 (1, 1, "Is k-means a classification algorithm?",
  "It assigns points to groups, so it sounds like classification.",
  "No. It's unsupervised clustering: there are no labels, and the groups it finds have no names until a person interprets them. Classification learns from labelled examples."),
 (1, 3, "When should you <em>not</em> use machine learning?",
  "Thinking ML is always the answer.",
  "When simple rules work and are easier to audit; when there's little or unrepresentative data; when every decision must be explained exactly; when mistakes are unacceptable and can't be caught; or when the data will change faster than you can retrain."),

 (2, 2, "Is cosine similarity a distance? What does it ignore?",
  "Treating 1 − cosine as a proper distance.",
  "It measures the angle between vectors and ignores their lengths, so (1, 1) and (100, 100) are identical to it. $1 - \\cos$ is not a true metric: it can violate the triangle inequality. Use it when direction matters more than magnitude, as with text embeddings."),
 (2, 2, "Can the nearest neighbour change if you switch from Euclidean (L2) to Manhattan (L1) distance?",
  "Assuming distances always agree on order.",
  "Yes. From (0, 0): the point (3, 0) is at L1 distance 3 and L2 distance 3; the point (2, 2) is at L1 4 but L2 2.83. L1 picks (3, 0); L2 picks (2, 2)."),
 (2, 1, "In NumPy, what's the difference between <code>A * B</code> and <code>A @ B</code>?",
  "Using <code>*</code> for matrix multiplication.",
  "<code>*</code> multiplies element by element (and broadcasts). <code>@</code> is matrix multiplication. A forward pass needs <code>@</code>; using <code>*</code> can silently give a wrong answer instead of an error if the shapes happen to broadcast."),
 (2, 2, "Why do we write predictions as $X\\mathbf{w}$ with examples as rows?",
  "Thinking the order is arbitrary.",
  "With $X$ of shape $(n, d)$ (one row per example) and $\\mathbf{w}$ of shape $(d,)$, $X\\mathbf{w}$ gives one prediction per row. It's a convention, but it fixes every other shape — in a neural layer, inputs $(n, d)$ times weights $(d, h)$ give outputs $(n, h)$."),
 (2, 3, "Two vectors have a dot product of 0. Are the features they represent unrelated?",
  "Equating perpendicular with independent.",
  "Only in a limited sense. A zero dot product means they're perpendicular. If the vectors are centred (mean subtracted), it means zero <em>correlation</em> — still only no linear relationship, not independence."),

 (3, 2, "The gradient is zero. Have you found the minimum?",
  "Assuming zero slope means the bottom.",
  "Not necessarily: it could be a maximum, a local minimum or a saddle point. In high-dimensional neural networks, saddle points are far more common than poor local minima, and noise in SGD helps escape them."),
 (3, 2, "ReLU isn't differentiable at 0. How can we train networks with it?",
  "Thinking every function must be differentiable everywhere.",
  "The kink is a single point that inputs almost never hit exactly. Frameworks simply use a derivative of 0 (or 1) there — a valid <em>subgradient</em> — and training works fine."),
 (3, 2, "Why does the gradient point in the direction of steepest <em>ascent</em>, and why do we subtract it?",
  "Thinking it points downhill.",
  "The directional slope along a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is $\\nabla f \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$, which is largest when $\\mathbf{u}$ points along $\\nabla f$. To decrease the loss we move the opposite way: $\\mathbf{w} \\leftarrow \\mathbf{w} - \\eta\\nabla L$."),
 (3, 3, "Why do frameworks compute gradients backwards (reverse mode) rather than forwards?",
  "Not knowing there's a choice.",
  "A network has one scalar loss and millions of parameters. Reverse mode gets all the partial derivatives of one output in a single backward pass, at about the cost of one forward pass. Forward mode would need one pass per parameter."),
 (3, 2, "What are numerical gradients good for, and why not train with them?",
  "Thinking they're as good as analytical ones.",
  "They're excellent for <em>checking</em> a hand-written gradient (gradient checking). But they need one or two extra evaluations per parameter and suffer from rounding error, so they're far too slow and imprecise for training."),

 (4, 2, "What's the difference between probability and likelihood?",
  "Using the words interchangeably.",
  "Same formula, different viewpoint. Probability fixes the parameters and asks how probable different data are (it sums to 1 over data). Likelihood fixes the observed data and asks how well different parameters explain it (it needn't sum to 1). Training by maximum likelihood picks the parameters under which the data are most probable."),
 (4, 2, "If two variables have zero correlation, are they independent?",
  "Yes, because nothing's going on.",
  "No. Correlation only measures linear relationships. For $x = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2$ and $y = x^2$, the correlation is exactly 0, yet $y$ is completely determined by $x$."),
 (4, 1, "Are mutually exclusive events independent?",
  "They seem to have nothing to do with each other.",
  "The opposite: if two events can't both happen (and each has non-zero probability), learning that one happened tells you the other didn't. That's strong dependence."),
 (4, 2, "Why divide by $n - 1$ instead of $n$ for a sample variance?",
  "\"It's just the formula.\"",
  "The sample mean is fitted to the same data, so deviations from it are slightly too small on average. Dividing by $n - 1$ (Bessel's correction) makes the estimate unbiased. For large $n$ the difference is tiny; NumPy's <code>np.var</code> divides by $n$ unless you set <code>ddof=1</code>."),
 (4, 2, "A test is 90% sensitive and the disease affects 1% of people. You test positive. Are you 90% likely to be ill?",
  "Confusing $P(+\\mid D)$ with $P(D\\mid +)$.",
  "No. With a 5% false-positive rate, $P(D \\mid +) \\approx 15\\%$, because false alarms from the many healthy people outnumber the true positives. This is the base-rate fallacy, and it applies to fraud and anomaly detectors too."),
 (4, 2, "What's the difference between standard deviation and standard error?",
  "Reporting the SD as the uncertainty of an average.",
  "SD describes the spread of individual values. SE describes the uncertainty of an estimate such as the mean: $\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$. With $\\sigma = 10$ and $n = 100$, individuals vary by about 10 but the mean is known to within about 1."),
 (4, 3, "A p-value is 0.03. Is there a 3% chance the null hypothesis is true?",
  "The most common statistics misconception.",
  "No. It's the probability of seeing data at least this extreme <em>if</em> the null hypothesis were true. It says nothing directly about how likely the hypothesis is, nor about the size of the effect."),
 (4, 1, "When is the median a better summary than the mean?",
  "Always reporting the mean.",
  "When data are skewed or contain outliers — incomes, house prices, response times. One extreme value can move the mean a lot (in Lesson 4, one typo moves it from 8 to 16) while the median stays put."),

 (5, 2, "Why do you need both a validation set and a test set?",
  "\"The test set is enough.\"",
  "Every time you use a set to choose something (model, hyperparameters, threshold), its score becomes optimistically biased. The validation set absorbs that tuning; the untouched test set gives the final honest estimate. Use it once."),
 (5, 3, "You select the 20 best features using all the data, then run 5-fold cross-validation. What's wrong?",
  "Thinking cross-validation protects you automatically.",
  "Leakage: feature selection has already seen the validation folds' labels, so the CV score is optimistic — sometimes wildly (it can find \"predictive\" features in pure noise). Every learned step, including selection, scaling and imputation, must happen inside each fold — use a Pipeline."),
 (5, 2, "Your training error is <em>higher</em> than your validation error. Is something broken?",
  "Assuming training error is always lower.",
  "Not necessarily. Regularisation that's only active in training (dropout, data augmentation) makes training look worse; a small or easy validation set can score well by luck. It can also signal leakage or duplicates between the sets, so check."),
 (5, 2, "Will collecting more data fix underfitting?",
  "\"More data always helps.\"",
  "Mostly no. More data reduces variance (overfitting). An underfitting model is too simple; it needs more features, a more flexible model or less regularisation."),
 (5, 2, "Why shouldn't you randomly shuffle time-series data before splitting?",
  "Using the default <code>train_test_split</code>.",
  "The model would train on the future and test on the past, so the score is optimistic. Split by time (train on earlier, validate on later) or use forward-chaining cross-validation (<code>TimeSeriesSplit</code>)."),
 (5, 2, "What's stratified splitting, and when do you need it?",
  "Using random splits for rare classes.",
  "Stratification keeps the class proportions the same in every split or fold. Without it, a fold of an imbalanced dataset might contain very few positives, making scores unstable."),
 (5, 2, "The same customer appears several times in your data. How should you split?",
  "Splitting rows randomly.",
  "Use group splitting (<code>GroupKFold</code>) so all rows of one customer go to the same side. Otherwise the model can recognise the customer instead of learning general patterns, and the validation score is inflated."),
 (5, 3, "What are the pros and cons of leave-one-out cross-validation?",
  "Thinking more folds is always better.",
  "Each model trains on nearly all the data, so the estimate has low bias. But it needs $n$ fits, and because the training sets are almost identical, the estimate can have high variance. 5- or 10-fold is usually the better trade-off."),

 (6, 1, "Is $\\hat{y} = w_1x + w_2x^2$ a linear regression?",
  "No, because it's a curve.",
  "Yes. \"Linear\" means linear in the parameters $w_1, w_2$. You add $x^2$ as a feature and use the same least-squares solution."),
 (6, 2, "You add a column of random noise to a linear regression. What happens to the training $R^2$?",
  "Expecting it to fall.",
  "It can only stay the same or rise: least squares can always set the new weight to zero, and usually finds a tiny improvement by fitting noise (0.8476 → 0.8478 in a quick test). That's why adjusted $R^2$, or held-out $R^2$, is used to compare models with different numbers of features."),
 (6, 2, "Can $R^2$ be negative?",
  "\"It's a squared number.\"",
  "On training data with an intercept, no. On test data, yes: $R^2 = 1 - \\text{SS}_{\\text{res}}/\\text{SS}_{\\text{tot}}$ goes below 0 whenever the model is worse than predicting the mean."),
 (6, 3, "A feature's coefficient is positive on its own but turns negative when you add another feature. Is that a bug?",
  "Assuming coefficients are fixed properties of a feature.",
  "No. A coefficient is the effect <em>holding the other features constant</em>. With two highly correlated features, the credit is split between them and signs can flip: in a quick test, a feature's slope was +1.97 alone and −1.04 alongside a correlated partner. Interpret coefficients with care, and use regularisation or drop redundant features."),
 (6, 3, "Does standardising features change the predictions of ordinary linear regression?",
  "Assuming scaling always matters.",
  "No. Plain least squares just rescales the coefficients to compensate, so predictions are identical (and verified so in Python). Scaling <em>does</em> change ridge and lasso, gradient-descent speed, and the comparability of coefficients."),
 (6, 2, "Does linear regression need the features or target to be normally distributed?",
  "A very common myth.",
  "No. Least squares works for any distributions. The normality assumption concerns the <em>residuals</em>, and only matters for exact confidence intervals and p-values — not for making predictions."),
 (6, 2, "When would you use gradient descent instead of the closed-form least-squares solution?",
  "\"The formula is always better.\"",
  "The formula needs $X^\\top X$ (size $d \\times d$) and solving it costs about $d^3$. With very many features or too much data for memory, iterative methods (gradient descent, SGD) are cheaper and can stream the data."),

 (7, 1, "What's the difference between an epoch, a batch and an iteration?",
  "Using epoch and iteration interchangeably.",
  "A batch is the set of examples for one update; an iteration is one update; an epoch is one pass over all training data. 1,000 examples with batch size 100 = 10 iterations per epoch."),
 (7, 2, "Does gradient descent always find the global minimum?",
  "\"Yes, if you run it long enough.\"",
  "Only for convex losses (linear and logistic regression), with a suitable learning rate. Neural-network losses are non-convex, so it finds a good-enough minimum depending on initialisation — which works surprisingly well in practice."),
 (7, 2, "Your loss becomes NaN after a few hundred steps. Name three likely causes.",
  "Blaming the data only.",
  "A learning rate that's too high (divergence), exploding gradients (try gradient clipping), and numerical problems such as $\\log(0)$ or dividing by zero (use the framework's built-in, numerically stable losses). Also check for NaNs in the inputs."),
 (7, 2, "Why does feature scaling speed up gradient descent?",
  "\"Scaling is only for distance-based models.\"",
  "Unscaled features make the loss surface a long, narrow valley; a learning rate small enough for the steep direction crawls along the flat one. In Lesson 7, multiplying $x$ by 10 made the original learning rate diverge within 5 steps."),
 (7, 2, "Why is mini-batch noise sometimes helpful?",
  "Thinking full-batch gradients are always better.",
  "The noise helps escape saddle points and sharp minima, and acts as a mild regulariser that often improves generalisation. Mini-batches are also much cheaper per step and use GPUs efficiently."),
 (7, 2, "Why shuffle the training data each epoch?",
  "Not shuffling at all.",
  "If data arrive sorted (all class A, then all class B), consecutive batches pull the weights back and forth. Shuffling makes each batch a fair sample, so gradient estimates are unbiased."),
 (7, 3, "How does increasing the batch size affect training, and what's often done with the learning rate?",
  "Assuming bigger batches are always better.",
  "Bigger batches give smoother gradients and faster epochs on parallel hardware, but fewer updates per epoch and sometimes worse generalisation. A common heuristic is to scale the learning rate up with the batch size, with a warm-up period."),

 (8, 1, "Why is it called logistic <em>regression</em> if it's used for classification?",
  "Thinking it's a misnomer.",
  "It is a regression on the <em>log-odds</em>: $\\ln\\frac{p}{1-p} = \\mathbf{w}\\cdot\\mathbf{x} + b$. The classification comes from thresholding the predicted probability."),
 (8, 2, "Why use log loss rather than MSE for logistic regression?",
  "\"MSE works for everything.\"",
  "MSE through a sigmoid gives a non-convex loss with tiny gradients when the model is confidently wrong, so learning stalls exactly when it should be fastest. Log loss is convex for logistic regression and punishes confident mistakes heavily."),
 (8, 2, "Is the decision boundary of logistic regression linear?",
  "\"No, the sigmoid is curved.\"",
  "Yes. The boundary is where $p = 0.5$, i.e. $\\mathbf{w}\\cdot\\mathbf{x} + b = 0$ — a straight line or flat plane. The sigmoid only bends the probabilities, not the boundary. Curved boundaries need extra features or another model."),
 (8, 3, "What happens to logistic regression on perfectly separable data without regularisation?",
  "Expecting a clean, finite answer.",
  "The weights grow without limit: making them larger always makes the probabilities more confident and the log loss smaller, approaching but never reaching 0. Regularisation (sklearn's default C = 1.0) keeps them finite."),
 (8, 2, "Your classifier outputs 0.9. Is the event 90% likely?",
  "Treating every score as a calibrated probability.",
  "Only if the model is calibrated. Logistic regression is usually close; boosted trees, SVMs and neural networks often aren't. Check with a reliability plot and fix with Platt scaling or isotonic regression."),
 (8, 2, "What's the relationship between softmax and sigmoid?",
  "Thinking they're unrelated.",
  "Softmax over two classes with scores $(z_1, z_2)$ gives $\\sigma(z_1 - z_2)$ for class 1. For scores (2, 0) both give 0.881."),
 (8, 2, "Should the classification threshold always be 0.5?",
  "Using the default.",
  "No. Choose it from the costs of each error and the class balance — a fraud system might flag at 0.1, a spam filter at 0.9 — using a validation set, never the test set."),

 (9, 1, "For cancer screening, would you optimise precision or recall? For a spam filter?",
  "Answering \"accuracy\".",
  "Cancer screening: recall — missing a cancer is far worse than an extra follow-up test. Spam filter: precision — losing a real email in the spam folder is worse than letting some spam through."),
 (9, 2, "Your fraud model has ROC-AUC 0.95 but the fraud team says most alerts are false. How can both be true?",
  "Trusting ROC-AUC on imbalanced data.",
  "FPR is divided by the huge number of normal transactions, so it looks tiny. With 10 frauds in 1,000 transactions, catching 9 at a 5% FPR still means about 49.5 false alarms: precision is about 15%. Use precision–recall curves and PR-AUC for rare positives."),
 (9, 2, "A model has ROC-AUC 0.3. Is it useless?",
  "Saying yes.",
  "It's worse than random, which means it ranks consistently in the wrong direction. Flip its scores and AUC becomes 0.7. Often a sign of inverted labels somewhere in the pipeline."),
 (9, 2, "Does changing the threshold change ROC-AUC?",
  "Thinking AUC depends on the operating point.",
  "No. AUC summarises all thresholds at once and depends only on how the scores rank positives against negatives. Precision, recall, F1 and accuracy do change with the threshold."),
 (9, 2, "What does F1 ignore, and when is that a problem?",
  "Using F1 everywhere.",
  "F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall and never uses true negatives. When correctly rejecting negatives matters, or both classes are important, consider balanced accuracy, MCC or per-class metrics."),
 (9, 2, "Macro-F1 is 0.55 but micro-F1 is 0.90. What does that tell you?",
  "Reporting whichever is higher.",
  "Micro-F1 is dominated by big classes; macro-F1 weights every class equally. The gap means the model does well on common classes and poorly on rare ones."),
 (9, 2, "When would you report RMSE rather than MAE?",
  "Treating them as interchangeable.",
  "When big errors are disproportionately bad (RMSE squares them). MAE treats every unit equally and is more robust to outliers. A large gap between RMSE and MAE means a few predictions are badly off."),

 (10, 2, "Why does L1 regularisation produce exactly-zero weights, but L2 doesn't?",
  "\"L1 is stronger.\"",
  "L1's penalty pulls each weight towards zero with a constant force, however small the weight, so small weights get pushed all the way to 0. L2's pull is proportional to the weight, so it fades as the weight shrinks and never quite reaches zero. Geometrically, the L1 constraint region has corners on the axes."),
 (10, 2, "Should you regularise the bias (intercept)?",
  "Penalising every parameter.",
  "Usually not. The intercept just shifts predictions to the right average level; shrinking it towards 0 biases every prediction and makes results depend on where the target's zero happens to be. Most libraries leave it unpenalised."),
 (10, 2, "Why must you scale features before ridge or lasso?",
  "Thinking regularisation is scale-free.",
  "The penalty acts on weight sizes, and a weight's size depends on its feature's units. Unscaled, a feature in small units needs a large weight and gets penalised more. In Lesson 10, measuring $x$ in units ten times larger changed how much of the weight survived $\\lambda = 1$ from 93% to 12%."),
 (10, 2, "Does more training data reduce bias or variance?",
  "\"Both.\"",
  "Variance. More data makes a flexible model's fit more stable. Bias comes from the model being too simple, and data doesn't change that."),
 (10, 2, "Lasso keeps one of two highly correlated features and drops the other. Is that a problem?",
  "Treating the selected feature as \"the important one\".",
  "Lasso picks between correlated features somewhat arbitrarily, and the choice can flip with small data changes. Elastic net (L1 + L2) tends to keep correlated features together. Don't read too much into which one survived."),
 (10, 2, "You choose $\\lambda$ by trying values on the test set. What's wrong?",
  "\"I'm only checking.\"",
  "Choosing $\\lambda$ with the test set leaks information into the model choice, so the test score is no longer an honest estimate. Use cross-validation on the training data (<code>RidgeCV</code>, <code>LassoCV</code>), then test once."),
 (10, 3, "Classical theory says test error rises once a model overfits. Why do huge neural networks sometimes generalise better as they grow?",
  "Applying the U-shaped curve everywhere.",
  "This is \"double descent\": test error rises near the point where the model can just fit the training data, then falls again as models grow far larger, helped by the implicit regularisation of SGD and good architectures. The bias–variance picture is still useful but not the whole story for very large models."),
 (10, 2, "Name three regularisers that aren't L1 or L2.",
  "Only knowing penalties.",
  "Early stopping, dropout, data augmentation; also limits like maximum tree depth or minimum leaf size, and simply using fewer features."),
]
